1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,388
Five a.m. Ho Chi Minh City still sleeps.

2
00:00:03,428 --> 00:00:06,926
But someone's already setting
furniture on the pavement:

3
00:00:06,966 --> 00:00:09,454
tiny plastic stools, knee-high or lower.

4
00:00:10,390 --> 00:00:13,158
Within an hour, half the
city will be sitting on them.

5
00:00:13,198 --> 00:00:16,746
Here people eat breakfast, drink
coffee, get haircuts, haggle, argue.

6
00:00:16,786 --> 00:00:19,139
You'll say: heat, low
prices, habit. All true.

7
00:00:19,179 --> 00:00:20,595
But there's one more reason.

8
00:00:20,635 --> 00:00:21,947
By morning you'll know it.

9
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:30,127
In ten months of twenty
twenty-six, one and a quarter

10
00:00:30,167 --> 00:00:32,247
million visitors from Russia flew in.

11
00:00:32,287 --> 00:00:36,144
In all of twenty twenty-four, about
two hundred and thirty thousand.

12
00:00:36,184 --> 00:00:38,952
Five times fewer. Warm,
tasty, one-dollar coffee.

13
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:45,610
This is a country with a skyscraper
four hundred and sixty-one metres tall.

14
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:48,749
And where a family of four can
live in five square metres.

15
00:00:48,789 --> 00:00:50,806
That's less than half a parking space.

16
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:07,121
Factory worker Mai, twenty, sends money
to her mum in the village monthly.

17
00:01:07,161 --> 00:01:09,048
She'll say something hard to forget.

18
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:13,528
Why is this country still struggling?

19
00:01:13,568 --> 00:01:15,454
And why do people love it from day one?

20
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:21,804
We'll spend twenty-four hours
here. Five a.m. to five a.m.

21
00:01:21,844 --> 00:01:25,769
Street food and markets, a sea of
motorbikes, rooms tourists never

22
00:01:25,809 --> 00:01:28,833
enter, and a night people
usually keep quiet about.

23
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:31,871
Today: Vietnam.

24
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,383
The people's names in this film come from
Vietnamese press and charity stories.

25
00:01:45,423 --> 00:01:48,295
On screen: not them, but
similar people and streets.

26
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:51,824
One of the first up is Lan.

27
00:01:51,864 --> 00:01:56,228
She rises at three a.m., by five she's
pushing her cart through the lanes of

28
00:01:56,268 --> 00:02:00,574
Saigon's District Four, making banh
mi: crusty baguette, meat, pâté, herbs.

29
00:02:00,614 --> 00:02:03,297
She sells till five p.m.
For thirty-plus years.

30
00:02:03,337 --> 00:02:05,151
Regulars cross the city for her.

31
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:13,165
When journalists wrote about her, Lan's
banh mi cost twenty thousand dong.

32
00:02:13,205 --> 00:02:16,152
Sounds like a salary, but it's
breakfast for under a dollar.

33
00:02:16,192 --> 00:02:19,090
Street food prices are watched
like bread prices elsewhere.

34
00:02:19,130 --> 00:02:22,575
When pho in Hanoi went from thirty
to thirty-five thousand a bowl, and

35
00:02:22,615 --> 00:02:25,762
forty in places, papers wrote:
'Thirty thousand pho is history.'

36
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:35,420
In twenty twenty-three, the
Michelin Guide came to Vietnam.

37
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:38,203
Four editions in a row,
and not a single banh mi.

38
00:02:38,243 --> 00:02:41,610
The inspectors never noticed
the country's most famous dish.

39
00:02:41,650 --> 00:02:45,755
Banh mi, it seems, didn't notice them
either. It's busy. There's a queue.

40
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:52,262
But honestly, street food is also risky.

41
00:02:52,302 --> 00:02:55,963
In November twenty twenty-five,
salmonella-tainted banh mi

42
00:02:56,003 --> 00:02:59,472
poisoned over three hundred
people in Ho Chi Minh City.

43
00:02:59,512 --> 00:03:03,108
Fresh, hot, cooked in front
of you: lower risk, not zero.

44
00:03:04,590 --> 00:03:08,531
By the river, beneath that same
skyscraper, people are dancing.

45
00:03:08,571 --> 00:03:12,134
They rise by five to fit in
exercise and dance before the

46
00:03:12,174 --> 00:03:15,041
heat: in courtyards,
parks, on the embankment.

47
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:19,693
The pavement here is
kitchen, dining room and gym.

48
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:24,227
The city fights back: in just four months
of twenty twenty-six, Ho Chi Minh City

49
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:28,024
recorded unpermitted pavement use
over thirty-three thousand times.

50
00:03:28,064 --> 00:03:30,177
Yet each morning, the stools are back.

51
00:03:33,330 --> 00:03:36,626
And coffee. In twenty twenty-five,
Vietnam exported almost

52
00:03:36,666 --> 00:03:38,811
nine billion dollars' worth, a record.

53
00:03:38,851 --> 00:03:41,744
At home, it drinks coffee
its own way: strong, with

54
00:03:41,784 --> 00:03:44,677
condensed milk and ice,
for about a dollar. Slowly.

55
00:03:44,717 --> 00:03:46,402
Sitting on a stool, of course.

56
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:52,748
Coffee's finished. In ten minutes,
this street will become a river.

57
00:03:55,350 --> 00:03:57,772
At seven, the city climbs onto motorbikes.

58
00:03:57,812 --> 00:04:00,527
Over seventy million
motorbikes are registered,

59
00:04:00,567 --> 00:04:02,696
for a hundred and two million people.

60
00:04:02,736 --> 00:04:06,799
To school, to work, with kids, rice
sacks and anything you can tie on.

61
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:13,075
In two thousand seven, helmets
became compulsory for everyone.

62
00:04:13,115 --> 00:04:16,949
Before the law, about forty percent
wore one; after, over ninety.

63
00:04:16,989 --> 00:04:19,810
A whole country put on
helmets almost overnight.

64
00:04:21,790 --> 00:04:23,410
Driver Nguyen is thirty-one.

65
00:04:23,450 --> 00:04:28,330
He drives an electric motorbike for a ride
app: out at dawn, done around seven p.m.

66
00:04:28,370 --> 00:04:33,131
On a good day he aims for about five
hundred thousand dong. Under twenty dollars.

67
00:04:33,171 --> 00:04:36,214
Vietnam has over seven
hundred thousand app drivers.

68
00:04:39,610 --> 00:04:42,132
For passengers, it's one of
the country's cheapest joys.

69
00:04:42,172 --> 00:04:44,282
About five kilometres
across town by motorbike:

70
00:04:44,322 --> 00:04:46,295
roughly twenty-five
thousand dong, a dollar.

71
00:04:46,335 --> 00:04:47,759
By car, two to three times more.

72
00:04:47,799 --> 00:04:49,909
Airport to central Saigon
by car: usually about

73
00:04:49,949 --> 00:04:51,693
five dollars, up to seven at rush hour.

74
00:04:51,733 --> 00:04:54,850
Twenty such bike rides make five
hundred thousand, Nguyen's good day.

75
00:04:54,890 --> 00:04:57,457
But the app takes a cut of
each, so he really needs more.

76
00:05:08,010 --> 00:05:11,527
Another driver, Nam, rode eighteen
hours a day over holidays.

77
00:05:11,567 --> 00:05:15,201
Then he cut back and told reporters:
'You can earn money again.

78
00:05:15,241 --> 00:05:17,243
Health is much harder to win back.'

79
00:05:20,590 --> 00:05:23,275
And the road is the day's
most dangerous place.

80
00:05:23,315 --> 00:05:26,059
Officially, in twenty
twenty-five, almost thirty

81
00:05:26,099 --> 00:05:28,204
people a day died on Vietnam's roads.

82
00:05:28,244 --> 00:05:31,046
Drink-driving is banned
outright: on a motorbike,

83
00:05:31,086 --> 00:05:33,133
the fine can reach ten million dong.

84
00:05:33,173 --> 00:05:35,163
More than the average monthly wage.

85
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:43,019
Now Saigon's top free attraction:
crossing the street. One rule: don't stop.

86
00:05:43,059 --> 00:05:46,191
Traffic flows around you
not because it sees you, but

87
00:05:46,231 --> 00:05:48,346
because it knows where you're going.

88
00:05:50,990 --> 00:05:52,466
We're right in the middle.

89
00:05:52,506 --> 00:05:54,390
No going back, no standing still.

90
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:58,122
The only safe move now is to hit
subscribe. Tested on ourselves.

91
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:04,004
The sea of motorbikes has
a price in the air too.

92
00:06:04,044 --> 00:06:07,967
In Hanoi, fine dust in twenty
twenty-five averaged about nine

93
00:06:08,007 --> 00:06:10,956
times the World Health
Organization guideline.

94
00:06:12,540 --> 00:06:14,345
But the country changes fast.

95
00:06:14,385 --> 00:06:19,243
In December twenty twenty-four, Ho Chi
Minh City opened its first metro line.

96
00:06:19,283 --> 00:06:22,487
Day one: about a hundred
and fifty thousand riders.

97
00:06:22,527 --> 00:06:24,459
True, the first month was free.

98
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:27,771
Motorbikes are going electric.

99
00:06:27,811 --> 00:06:31,492
In twenty twenty-five, the biggest local
maker sold over four hundred thousand

100
00:06:31,532 --> 00:06:34,974
e-motorbikes, and Vietnam is now among
the world's three biggest markets.

101
00:06:35,014 --> 00:06:38,551
Old petrol motorbikes were to be banned
from central Hanoi in summer twenty

102
00:06:38,591 --> 00:06:41,461
twenty-six, but the ban was
postponed to twenty twenty-eight.

103
00:06:41,501 --> 00:06:43,226
Helmets took one night. Petrol won't.

104
00:06:48,950 --> 00:06:53,161
Now let's step off the road, to where
everything has two prices. One for locals.

105
00:06:53,201 --> 00:06:54,118
The other for you.

106
00:06:58,450 --> 00:07:02,962
Vietnamese market rule one: the first price
isn't the price. It asks: who are you?

107
00:07:04,910 --> 00:07:08,534
Trang has sold clothes at Ben
Thanh Market for thirty years.

108
00:07:08,574 --> 00:07:13,297
Her takings, she says, have more than
halved: 'Many visitors, very few buyers.

109
00:07:13,337 --> 00:07:14,396
Most just browse.'

110
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:18,810
Tourists fear overpaying. Rightly.

111
00:07:18,850 --> 00:07:21,973
In twenty twenty-three,
one stall asked a Japanese

112
00:07:22,013 --> 00:07:25,894
tourist ten times the price for
socks. It was shut for a week.

113
00:07:27,630 --> 00:07:29,594
The commonest trap is in your wallet.

114
00:07:29,634 --> 00:07:32,302
The twenty thousand and
five hundred thousand dong

115
00:07:32,342 --> 00:07:34,143
notes are both blue, both plastic.

116
00:07:34,183 --> 00:07:37,609
Mix them up and you overpay about
eighteen dollars for a coffee.

117
00:07:37,649 --> 00:07:40,805
The five hundred thousand is
bigger and shows Ho Chi Minh's

118
00:07:40,845 --> 00:07:44,163
birthplace; the twenty thousand,
Hoi An's old Japanese Bridge.

119
00:07:50,350 --> 00:07:53,960
The locals' market is Binh Tay,
in Cholon, the Chinese quarter.

120
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:58,363
A Chinese merchant paid for it and gave
it to the city almost a century ago.

121
00:07:58,403 --> 00:08:02,650
From here, goods go all over the south,
bought by the sack, not the piece.

122
00:08:07,730 --> 00:08:09,512
The market's flip side: bags.

123
00:08:09,552 --> 00:08:13,408
Vietnam discards about three
million tonnes of plastic a year.

124
00:08:13,448 --> 00:08:18,121
Estimates say three hundred to seven
hundred thousand tonnes reach the sea.

125
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:22,039
And where there's rubbish
and food, there are rats.

126
00:08:22,079 --> 00:08:25,984
'The city may look magnificent, but
it has so many rats,' the founder of a

127
00:08:26,024 --> 00:08:29,396
rat-catching firm told a Vietnamese
paper back in twenty twenty.

128
00:08:29,436 --> 00:08:32,541
In Ho Chi Minh City, professional
rat catchers are hired by

129
00:08:32,581 --> 00:08:35,154
restaurants, supermarkets,
offices and factories.

130
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:42,033
There's a floating morning market too.

131
00:08:42,073 --> 00:08:45,997
At Cai Rang, in the Mekong Delta,
they sell straight from boats.

132
00:08:46,037 --> 00:08:50,208
A pole above each boat shows the
goods: pineapple, pumpkin, cassava.

133
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:53,563
But the boats are dwindling.

134
00:08:53,603 --> 00:08:56,477
About two hundred now, under
half the nineties' number.

135
00:08:56,517 --> 00:09:00,397
Goods now travel by bridges and roads,
in trucks. Want to see this market?

136
00:09:00,437 --> 00:09:01,298
Don't put it off.

137
00:09:07,990 --> 00:09:11,658
By noon, we'll learn this country's
great secret. It's very pleasant.

138
00:09:11,698 --> 00:09:14,345
Until you look from the
other side of the counter.

139
00:09:17,010 --> 00:09:18,185
At noon the city slows.

140
00:09:18,225 --> 00:09:21,515
Some nap on their motorbikes,
others in hammocks behind stalls.

141
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:27,795
Meanwhile, the tourist discovers
Vietnam's great secret: what counts

142
00:09:27,835 --> 00:09:30,806
as a small luxury at home
costs a few dollars here.

143
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:35,642
Vinh had cut hair in one
District One alley for twenty

144
00:09:35,682 --> 00:09:37,940
years when a newspaper wrote about him.

145
00:09:37,980 --> 00:09:40,355
He came from Da Nang dreaming of success.

146
00:09:40,395 --> 00:09:45,069
Every morning, seven days a week, he
brought his whole kit by motorbike: a small

147
00:09:45,109 --> 00:09:49,193
mirror, scissors, clippers, and a
chair about twenty years old itself.

148
00:09:49,233 --> 00:09:52,021
Americans, Brits, Germans
and Koreans sat in it.

149
00:09:52,061 --> 00:09:53,907
The paper doesn't say his price.

150
00:09:53,947 --> 00:09:57,206
A barbershop cut costs sixty
to a hundred thousand dong.

151
00:09:57,246 --> 00:10:01,743
In Germany, says Vietnamese radio, about
twenty euros, six to ten times more.

152
00:10:08,580 --> 00:10:11,805
Hair wash with massage: a
ritual, sometimes an hour, that

153
00:10:11,845 --> 00:10:14,325
foreigners describe at
home like a landmark.

154
00:10:14,365 --> 00:10:17,418
In Hoi An, a tailored suit
is usually a hundred to two

155
00:10:17,458 --> 00:10:19,939
hundred-plus dollars,
in days, two fittings.

156
00:10:19,979 --> 00:10:22,745
Dental care, says that
radio, costs three to four

157
00:10:22,785 --> 00:10:25,151
times less than in Australia or Singapore.

158
00:10:29,540 --> 00:10:32,034
One price rule to remember
from this film: anything

159
00:10:32,074 --> 00:10:34,170
done for you by hand,
right here, is cheap.

160
00:10:34,210 --> 00:10:37,152
Food, coffee, haircuts, massage,
tailoring, motorbike taxis.

161
00:10:37,192 --> 00:10:39,934
Lunch at a table in an ordinary
café: about two dollars.

162
00:10:39,974 --> 00:10:43,809
Dinner for two, mid-range restaurant:
about twenty-five dollars, drinks extra.

163
00:10:43,849 --> 00:10:47,238
With wine, it's another story: good
wine here is almost all imported.

164
00:10:54,350 --> 00:10:58,248
But anything shipped from afar
or bought for years is expensive.

165
00:10:58,288 --> 00:11:02,309
In twenty twenty-three, the trade
ministry admitted cars here cost

166
00:11:02,349 --> 00:11:06,062
almost twice Thailand's prices,
mostly due to taxes and fees.

167
00:11:06,102 --> 00:11:08,523
Only nine families in a hundred own one.

168
00:11:08,563 --> 00:11:11,846
A year of senior school at
Ho Chi Minh City's priciest

169
00:11:11,886 --> 00:11:14,614
international schools:
almost a billion dong.

170
00:11:14,654 --> 00:11:18,121
About nine years of the average
wage. So labour is cheap.

171
00:11:18,161 --> 00:11:20,767
Cars, schooling and
square metres are dear.

172
00:11:20,807 --> 00:11:23,351
What a square metre costs, we'll soon see.

173
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:30,298
In peak heat, many
tourists head underground.

174
00:11:30,338 --> 00:11:32,913
Some seventy kilometres from
the centre lie the Cu Chi

175
00:11:32,953 --> 00:11:35,430
tunnels, where guerrillas
hid and fought in the war.

176
00:11:35,470 --> 00:11:37,560
The network ran over
two hundred kilometres.

177
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:40,416
Visitors now crawl through
sections widened for them, and a

178
00:11:40,456 --> 00:11:42,885
nearby range lets you fire
real weapons, for extra.

179
00:11:42,925 --> 00:11:45,209
Strange: here war is both
museum and attraction.

180
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:55,304
In the city itself: the
War Remnants Museum.

181
00:11:55,344 --> 00:11:58,170
A ticket is a couple of
dollars; since nineteen

182
00:11:58,210 --> 00:12:01,341
seventy-five, over twenty-five
million have visited.

183
00:12:01,381 --> 00:12:03,110
One hall covers Agent Orange.

184
00:12:03,150 --> 00:12:05,428
We'll meet that name again by the sea.

185
00:12:07,780 --> 00:12:11,193
Half a century ago, the war here
had just ended. Then, a leap.

186
00:12:11,233 --> 00:12:15,146
From twenty ten to twenty twenty-five,
the share of poor Vietnamese, by

187
00:12:15,186 --> 00:12:18,599
World Bank measures, fell from
fourteen percent to under four.

188
00:12:18,639 --> 00:12:21,216
But escaping poverty
isn't yet a carefree life.

189
00:12:25,690 --> 00:12:27,488
Now, these prices from behind the counter.

190
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:33,260
Average pay: about three hundred
and fifty dollars a month.

191
00:12:33,300 --> 00:12:36,277
Almost two thirds work with
no contract or insurance;

192
00:12:36,317 --> 00:12:38,612
under half of older people get a pension.

193
00:12:38,652 --> 00:12:41,630
In spring twenty twenty-six,
a union survey found one

194
00:12:41,670 --> 00:12:45,616
worker in five can't get by without
overtime. Up noticeably in a year.

195
00:12:50,010 --> 00:12:53,431
Where does the money go? Food,
children, parents back in the village.

196
00:12:53,471 --> 00:12:56,491
And a roof overhead. Here begins
the hardest part of our day.

197
00:13:02,090 --> 00:13:05,082
This chapter is based on
Vietnamese journalists' reports.

198
00:13:06,450 --> 00:13:10,793
Ngoc, a textile worker, came from Can Tho,
in the Mekong Delta, over ten years ago.

199
00:13:10,833 --> 00:13:13,539
With her husband and two
kids, she rents five square

200
00:13:13,579 --> 00:13:15,810
metres: about two metres
by two and a half.

201
00:13:15,850 --> 00:13:18,767
Rent: one and a half million
dong, around sixty dollars.

202
00:13:18,807 --> 00:13:22,041
Both kids are at school; they
chose the cheapest room to save.

203
00:13:30,110 --> 00:13:31,668
Same report: Huong, sixty.

204
00:13:31,708 --> 00:13:34,064
Her room: two metres by one and a half.

205
00:13:34,104 --> 00:13:35,784
Almost exactly a bed's size.

206
00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:42,379
These aren't one-offs. Twenty twenty-one
data showed workers in such rooming

207
00:13:42,419 --> 00:13:44,852
houses averaged under
three square metres each.

208
00:13:44,892 --> 00:13:47,168
The average Vietnamese
has over twenty-five.

209
00:13:47,208 --> 00:13:51,062
And the Ho Chi Minh City real estate
association estimates about one point

210
00:13:51,102 --> 00:13:54,640
eight million people live in the
city's mini-houses and micro-flats.

211
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:56,061
More than all of Barcelona.

212
00:13:59,640 --> 00:14:02,417
One is Mai, twenty, from
Ca Mau in the far south.

213
00:14:02,457 --> 00:14:05,119
She works in a factory
zone on the city's edge.

214
00:14:05,159 --> 00:14:09,373
Pay: six to seven million dong, plus
one or two overtime. Rent: a million.

215
00:14:09,413 --> 00:14:12,650
Monthly, she sends her mum
one to one and a half million.

216
00:14:19,120 --> 00:14:22,224
She told a local columnist:
'If I can only send Mum five

217
00:14:22,264 --> 00:14:24,976
hundred thousand, I'd
rather go home and peddle.'

218
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:30,784
Five hundred thousand dong
is under twenty dollars.

219
00:14:30,824 --> 00:14:33,401
For Mai, it's the line
between the city and home.

220
00:14:42,180 --> 00:14:46,461
Why not a bigger room? Each extra
million on rent is one not sent home.

221
00:14:46,501 --> 00:14:50,234
Buying is nearly impossible: a
new flat in central Ho Chi Minh

222
00:14:50,274 --> 00:14:53,459
City costs over ninety
million dong per square metre.

223
00:14:53,499 --> 00:14:56,015
About ten average salaries. For one metre.

224
00:15:00,050 --> 00:15:04,661
Housing analysts reckon city residents
need fifty-three years' income for a home.

225
00:15:04,701 --> 00:15:06,441
Without spending a single dong.

226
00:15:08,070 --> 00:15:09,968
Not everyone lives like this.

227
00:15:10,008 --> 00:15:13,176
The typical town house
is narrow, tall and deep.

228
00:15:13,216 --> 00:15:18,455
Downstairs, a shop or café by day;
upstairs, several generations of one family.

229
00:15:19,270 --> 00:15:22,553
Where land is especially dear,
houses squeeze into any gap.

230
00:15:22,593 --> 00:15:25,369
The press found Ho Chi
Minh City homes just over a

231
00:15:25,409 --> 00:15:27,847
metre from front to
back, some half a metre.

232
00:15:27,887 --> 00:15:30,833
Leftover plots: a road or
canal was widened, the plot

233
00:15:30,873 --> 00:15:33,592
nearly vanished, but the
house and people stayed.

234
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:42,547
Weddings turn the street into a
living room: a tent in the alley.

235
00:15:42,587 --> 00:15:45,537
In Thu Duc, a family put a
wedding tent, three by ten

236
00:15:45,577 --> 00:15:47,681
metres, on a busy road, and got fined.

237
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:54,008
Crowding has a darker price.

238
00:15:54,048 --> 00:15:58,311
In September twenty twenty-three, a
mini-apartment building caught fire in Hanoi.

239
00:15:58,351 --> 00:16:02,614
Fifty-six people died. Fire engines couldn't
get close: the alley was too narrow.

240
00:16:02,654 --> 00:16:06,226
The owner, it turned out, had at
least eight more buildings like it.

241
00:16:11,290 --> 00:16:14,876
Ho Chi Minh City itself is
slowly sinking: two to five

242
00:16:14,916 --> 00:16:18,637
centimetres a year on average,
seven to eight in places.

243
00:16:18,677 --> 00:16:20,517
A cramped city, sinking too.

244
00:16:22,080 --> 00:16:23,967
After that chapter, we need the sea.

245
00:16:24,007 --> 00:16:25,947
But here, even the sea has two sides.

246
00:16:28,620 --> 00:16:29,935
Six p.m., by the sea.

247
00:16:29,975 --> 00:16:34,708
Yes, we bent time a bit: here an evening
holds more than a week back home.

248
00:16:34,748 --> 00:16:37,224
Nha Trang. Same stools, facing the sea.

249
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:45,719
Russia is now Vietnam's third-largest
source of tourists, after China and Korea.

250
00:16:45,759 --> 00:16:48,713
On some Nha Trang streets,
signs were only in foreign

251
00:16:48,753 --> 00:16:50,747
languages: Russian, Korean, English.

252
00:16:50,787 --> 00:16:53,910
In summer twenty twenty-six,
the authorities stepped in:

253
00:16:53,950 --> 00:16:56,452
by law, Vietnamese is
required on signs. Yes.

254
00:16:56,492 --> 00:16:59,277
Signs in Vietnam were asked
to include Vietnamese.

255
00:17:02,170 --> 00:17:04,904
For thirty years, one fruit
seller has walked Nha Trang beach.

256
00:17:04,944 --> 00:17:06,961
She's fifty-eight, and
taught herself English,

257
00:17:07,001 --> 00:17:08,751
Russian and Chinese, from her customers.

258
00:17:08,791 --> 00:17:12,017
Her clip, chanting fruit names in
English, has nearly four million views.

259
00:17:12,057 --> 00:17:13,940
In summer twenty
twenty-six, her goods were

260
00:17:13,980 --> 00:17:15,909
confiscated: selling
on the beach is banned.

261
00:17:15,949 --> 00:17:19,398
They were returned, on a promise not to
trade there again. Tourists adore her.

262
00:17:19,438 --> 00:17:20,114
The rules don't.

263
00:17:29,470 --> 00:17:34,555
Vietnamese press puts it bluntly: hospitality
shouldn't be confused with tolerance.

264
00:17:34,595 --> 00:17:39,187
People here are tired of foreigners
who break the rules, and say so openly.

265
00:17:40,090 --> 00:17:44,308
Nha Trang's big attraction: Hon
Tre island, opposite the city.

266
00:17:44,348 --> 00:17:48,016
A cable car crosses the sea
to a big park with slides.

267
00:17:48,056 --> 00:17:51,175
Ticket plus the crossing:
about forty dollars.

268
00:17:51,215 --> 00:17:54,609
Roughly a ninth of Vietnam's
average monthly wage.

269
00:17:56,360 --> 00:17:59,654
Visitors from Russia get up
to forty-five days visa-free.

270
00:17:59,694 --> 00:18:02,638
Russian bank cards barely
work, so they bring cash.

271
00:18:02,678 --> 00:18:06,089
Numbeo users put a month in Ho
Chi Minh City, without rent,

272
00:18:06,129 --> 00:18:08,780
at almost five hundred
dollars for one person.

273
00:18:11,990 --> 00:18:15,850
A bowl of pho with Japanese
wagyu for fourteen dollars at

274
00:18:15,890 --> 00:18:20,434
Nha Trang airport sparked a nationwide
row. Vietnam forgives a lot.

275
00:18:20,474 --> 00:18:24,471
But not pho priced like nine
street bowls. Even with wagyu.

276
00:18:25,730 --> 00:18:28,781
The rainy season here
isn't a guidebook footnote.

277
00:18:28,821 --> 00:18:33,072
In November twenty twenty-five,
floods in south-central Vietnam took

278
00:18:33,112 --> 00:18:36,416
ninety-eight lives, over
ten in Nha Trang's province.

279
00:18:36,456 --> 00:18:38,498
Check the calendar when you book.

280
00:18:39,930 --> 00:18:44,595
Further north is Da Nang, a city
more and more digital nomads choose.

281
00:18:44,635 --> 00:18:50,255
On weekends at nine p.m., a dragon-shaped
bridge breathes fire and water. For free.

282
00:19:00,920 --> 00:19:03,836
Above Da Nang, in the
mountains: Ba Na Hills.

283
00:19:03,876 --> 00:19:07,251
A cable car almost six
kilometres long takes you up.

284
00:19:07,291 --> 00:19:12,440
Guinness still lists it as the world's
longest non-stop single-track cable car.

285
00:19:13,850 --> 00:19:15,523
At the top is the Golden Bridge.

286
00:19:15,563 --> 00:19:19,004
A hundred and fifty metres of
golden walkway over a kilometre up,

287
00:19:19,044 --> 00:19:21,734
seemingly held from below
by two giant stone hands.

288
00:19:21,774 --> 00:19:25,803
Now a secret: the hands aren't stone,
and they don't really hold the bridge.

289
00:19:25,843 --> 00:19:29,873
Inside is steel mesh and fibreglass,
and the 'stone' was painted by artists.

290
00:19:38,490 --> 00:19:42,320
Ticket plus cable car: about a
million dong, almost forty dollars.

291
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:45,487
Roughly what factory worker
Mai sends her mum monthly.

292
00:19:47,670 --> 00:19:49,047
Now, what the ads leave out.

293
00:19:49,087 --> 00:19:51,982
Soil at the former American
air base, now Da Nang airport,

294
00:19:52,022 --> 00:19:54,209
was cleaned of dioxin
until twenty eighteen.

295
00:19:54,249 --> 00:19:57,093
The poison came from Agent
Orange, the herbicide American

296
00:19:57,133 --> 00:19:59,118
forces sprayed on the jungle in the war.

297
00:19:59,158 --> 00:20:02,761
Researchers estimate two to almost
five million Vietnamese were exposed.

298
00:20:02,801 --> 00:20:05,950
Vietnam cites three million
victims; the US side disputes that.

299
00:20:12,740 --> 00:20:15,158
Some seventeen percent of Vietnam is still

300
00:20:15,198 --> 00:20:17,849
contaminated with
unexploded bombs and shells.

301
00:20:17,889 --> 00:20:21,185
Since the war, they've killed
over forty thousand people.

302
00:20:21,225 --> 00:20:22,764
For tourists, it's history.

303
00:20:22,804 --> 00:20:25,690
For a Vietnamese family:
a grandfather lost in the

304
00:20:25,730 --> 00:20:27,855
war, a neighbour blown up in a field.

305
00:20:30,550 --> 00:20:31,906
Back to Ho Chi Minh City.

306
00:20:31,946 --> 00:20:33,413
The neon is just coming on.

307
00:20:38,940 --> 00:20:41,880
Nine p.m. Bui Vien Street,
pedestrian since twenty seventeen.

308
00:20:41,920 --> 00:20:43,980
Evenings here are loud:
music, neon, touts.

309
00:20:44,020 --> 00:20:46,618
This morning, workers
breakfasted on these stools; now

310
00:20:46,658 --> 00:20:48,572
tourists drink beer on them, mid-street.

311
00:20:48,612 --> 00:20:49,891
The stool works two shifts.

312
00:20:49,931 --> 00:20:53,702
A mug of local draught: around twenty
to thirty thousand dong, about a dollar.

313
00:20:53,742 --> 00:20:55,998
And in beer, Vietnam is
in the world's top ten.

314
00:21:05,740 --> 00:21:07,391
Karaoke is a whole other song.

315
00:21:07,431 --> 00:21:10,209
Since December twenty
twenty-five, loud karaoke is

316
00:21:10,249 --> 00:21:12,294
fined at any hour, not just at night.

317
00:21:12,334 --> 00:21:15,168
Journalists wrote New Year's
Eve in one residential

318
00:21:15,208 --> 00:21:17,592
district was noticeably
quieter after that.

319
00:21:17,632 --> 00:21:21,988
Neighbours heard each other for the first
time in years. Without a microphone.

320
00:21:25,490 --> 00:21:28,073
While some party, others
are just heading to work.

321
00:21:28,113 --> 00:21:31,010
His name is Cong Thai, he's
twenty-five, from the Mekong

322
00:21:31,050 --> 00:21:33,370
Delta, and he used to
work in a shoe factory.

323
00:21:33,410 --> 00:21:37,462
Now he's a night courier: out after nine
p.m., delivering orders till morning.

324
00:21:37,502 --> 00:21:39,875
On a good night he makes
up to a million dong.

325
00:21:39,915 --> 00:21:41,553
'Night is golden time,' he says.

326
00:21:50,910 --> 00:21:53,755
At night, people here talk loudly
about almost everything. Almost.

327
00:21:53,795 --> 00:21:57,077
In Reporters Without Borders' twenty
twenty-six press freedom index, Vietnam

328
00:21:57,117 --> 00:21:59,831
ranks one hundred and seventy-fourth
of one hundred and eighty.

329
00:21:59,871 --> 00:22:02,322
Since late twenty twenty-four,
social media accounts must

330
00:22:02,362 --> 00:22:04,289
be verified with a
Vietnamese number to post.

331
00:22:04,329 --> 00:22:05,950
The authorities say it's for security.

332
00:22:05,990 --> 00:22:08,179
Rights groups: so that no
anonymous critics remain.

333
00:22:15,410 --> 00:22:17,955
Massage here is almost
always just massage.

334
00:22:17,995 --> 00:22:20,661
But sometimes that sign
hides something else.

335
00:22:20,701 --> 00:22:24,268
In January twenty twenty-six,
Hanoi fined a tout who offered

336
00:22:24,308 --> 00:22:27,274
foreigners drugs to lure
them into such a 'salon'.

337
00:22:27,314 --> 00:22:29,138
Let's talk about this honestly.

338
00:22:34,940 --> 00:22:36,957
Prostitution is illegal in Vietnam.

339
00:22:36,997 --> 00:22:40,719
Since two thousand three, the law
has called it a 'social evil'.

340
00:22:40,759 --> 00:22:44,598
Buying such services means a fine,
and foreigners can be deported.

341
00:22:44,638 --> 00:22:47,654
With anyone under eighteen,
it's a criminal offence.

342
00:22:50,780 --> 00:22:52,649
But a ban doesn't mean it isn't there.

343
00:22:52,689 --> 00:22:55,915
Ho Chi Minh City's authorities
admit it hides behind spa, karaoke

344
00:22:55,955 --> 00:22:58,377
and guesthouse signs, and
increasingly in phones.

345
00:22:58,417 --> 00:23:00,889
Nobody knows exactly how
many people do this work.

346
00:23:00,929 --> 00:23:03,301
Official figures say
eleven to sixteen thousand.

347
00:23:03,341 --> 00:23:06,165
The International Labour
Organization, in twenty sixteen,

348
00:23:06,205 --> 00:23:08,576
cited an estimate of about
one hundred thousand.

349
00:23:14,760 --> 00:23:15,724
Why are they there?

350
00:23:15,764 --> 00:23:20,110
The same organization's researchers
interviewed seventy-three people in this field.

351
00:23:20,150 --> 00:23:21,695
Only one was deceived into it.

352
00:23:21,735 --> 00:23:25,869
The rest came on their own, almost all
for one reason: the family needed money.

353
00:23:25,909 --> 00:23:28,141
Remember: two thirds
work with no contract.

354
00:23:28,181 --> 00:23:31,100
And city pay is almost one
and a half times village pay.

355
00:23:37,080 --> 00:23:39,232
But 'on their own' doesn't mean 'free'.

356
00:23:39,272 --> 00:23:42,830
They described ten to twelve hour
workdays, violence or constant

357
00:23:42,870 --> 00:23:45,529
fear of it, and owners
who take their documents.

358
00:23:49,400 --> 00:23:50,969
Sometimes the trap is debt.

359
00:23:51,009 --> 00:23:54,604
In twenty twenty-two, Saigon
police freed eleven young women.

360
00:23:54,644 --> 00:23:58,061
A recruiter 'bought' their
debts, promised city jobs, then

361
00:23:58,101 --> 00:24:00,623
made them sign debt
papers and work it off.

362
00:24:00,663 --> 00:24:02,470
Six came from the Mekong Delta.

363
00:24:05,080 --> 00:24:07,258
Who are the clients?
Foreigners and locals alike.

364
00:24:07,298 --> 00:24:10,924
Police bust networks serving tourists,
and back in the two thousands, researchers

365
00:24:10,964 --> 00:24:14,364
described local men treating it as
'entertainment' after a drinking session.

366
00:24:14,404 --> 00:24:16,989
And at the top, there's almost
no punishment: the US State

367
00:24:17,029 --> 00:24:20,067
Department noted that for twenty
twenty-four, Vietnam reported not a

368
00:24:20,107 --> 00:24:22,466
single case against officials
who helped traffickers.

369
00:24:29,140 --> 00:24:33,559
Women pay the price. HIV is many times
more common among them than others.

370
00:24:33,599 --> 00:24:37,716
Researchers say stigma is the biggest
barrier to a normal life again.

371
00:24:40,340 --> 00:24:43,536
Graver still, reaching far beyond
the night streets: human trafficking.

372
00:24:43,576 --> 00:24:46,498
In twenty twenty-four, the
authorities officially identified five

373
00:24:46,538 --> 00:24:49,187
hundred victims, and rights
groups are sure there are more.

374
00:24:49,227 --> 00:24:51,922
Women from mountain villages
are sold into China as 'wives'.

375
00:24:51,962 --> 00:24:54,702
And in twenty nineteen, in
England, the bodies of thirty-nine

376
00:24:54,742 --> 00:24:57,893
Vietnamese were found in a refrigerated
lorry. They were seeking work.

377
00:25:04,350 --> 00:25:06,058
A charity calls one survivor Van.

378
00:25:06,098 --> 00:25:09,396
She dreamed of a clothes shop;
her two-year-old waited at home.

379
00:25:09,436 --> 00:25:12,257
She went to China for stock
with a neighbour's friend.

380
00:25:12,297 --> 00:25:15,012
In three months, she was
sold as a wife three times.

381
00:25:15,052 --> 00:25:19,092
In spring twenty sixteen, Hanoi's Blue
Dragon rescued her, bringing her home.

382
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:26,620
Her daughter was very small then.

383
00:25:26,660 --> 00:25:29,687
The charity says she knew only
one thing: Mum was home again.

384
00:25:32,770 --> 00:25:36,715
Since July twenty twenty-five, Vietnam
has a new anti-trafficking law.

385
00:25:36,755 --> 00:25:39,561
Blue Dragon has rescued
about two thousand people.

386
00:25:39,601 --> 00:25:43,090
And in twenty twenty-five, the
children's helpline one one one

387
00:25:43,130 --> 00:25:45,879
took over three hundred
and fifty thousand calls.

388
00:25:47,730 --> 00:25:49,801
For future tourists, briefly and plainly.

389
00:25:49,841 --> 00:25:52,376
It's illegal: a fine and
deportation. It's a risk.

390
00:25:52,416 --> 00:25:56,393
In twenty eighteen in Ho Chi Minh City,
a woman a foreigner met online drugged

391
00:25:56,433 --> 00:25:59,843
him and took over two hundred and
fifty million dong from his home.

392
00:25:59,883 --> 00:26:02,315
And too often, behind it
lies someone's poverty,

393
00:26:02,355 --> 00:26:04,581
someone's debt or
someone's lack of freedom.

394
00:26:11,290 --> 00:26:13,517
We don't show these
streets or these women.

395
00:26:13,557 --> 00:26:15,257
It's their life, not our footage.

396
00:26:15,297 --> 00:26:19,634
We'll say just one thing: researchers say
many of them came to the big city for the

397
00:26:19,674 --> 00:26:23,272
same reason as everyone we saw today:
to earn money and send it home.

398
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:32,577
Five a.m. The day comes full circle.

399
00:26:32,617 --> 00:26:37,610
Cleaners sweep Bui Vien, the riverside
dances again, and Lan's been up two hours.

400
00:26:39,240 --> 00:26:42,396
Now, the opening question: why
is all of life out on the street?

401
00:26:42,436 --> 00:26:44,694
Climate, habit, cheap
food: true, but not all.

402
00:26:44,734 --> 00:26:47,291
If your home, like Ngoc's,
is five square metres for

403
00:26:47,331 --> 00:26:49,239
four, the pavement is your living room.

404
00:26:49,279 --> 00:26:52,385
Kitchen, dining room, where you
meet friends and hold weddings.

405
00:26:52,425 --> 00:26:53,983
Your furniture: a plastic stool.

406
00:27:01,240 --> 00:27:03,879
And when a tourist sits
on one with a one-dollar

407
00:27:03,919 --> 00:27:05,888
coffee, they're unknowingly a guest.

408
00:27:05,928 --> 00:27:07,841
In the only big room this city has.

409
00:27:12,740 --> 00:27:14,421
How many metres does a person need?

410
00:27:14,461 --> 00:27:17,911
Tolstoy's hero chased land all his
life; in the end, six feet sufficed.

411
00:27:17,951 --> 00:27:20,369
Ho Chi Minh City answers
otherwise: not the metres

412
00:27:20,409 --> 00:27:22,090
inside, but what's beyond the door.

413
00:27:22,130 --> 00:27:25,138
Five metres for a family is too
little; no smile changes that.

414
00:27:25,178 --> 00:27:27,597
Here they know what we
forget behind walls: a room

415
00:27:27,637 --> 00:27:29,416
can be cramped, the street is shared.

416
00:27:38,990 --> 00:27:40,522
But there's a second answer.

417
00:27:40,562 --> 00:27:44,060
Once a year, at Tet, the Vietnamese
New Year, the city empties.

418
00:27:44,100 --> 00:27:46,699
People take motorbikes,
trains and planes home.

419
00:27:46,739 --> 00:27:50,910
Stools vanish from many pavements for
days: nobody's there to set them out.

420
00:27:54,310 --> 00:27:57,781
Worker Hoan hadn't taken his wife
and three kids home for Tet in

421
00:27:57,821 --> 00:28:00,415
five years: tickets: about
fifteen million dong.

422
00:28:00,455 --> 00:28:03,706
In twenty twenty-six, the union
gave them a free train home.

423
00:28:03,746 --> 00:28:07,382
'Without that help, who knows when
we'd next have Tet with family.'

424
00:28:12,270 --> 00:28:14,334
So the pavement's just a living room.

425
00:28:14,374 --> 00:28:18,543
Their real home is where they came
from: Can Tho for Ngoc, Ca Mau for Mai.

426
00:28:18,583 --> 00:28:22,012
For it, many live in five metres
and send money home monthly.

427
00:28:26,980 --> 00:28:30,142
We saw all day why it's
hard. Why people fall for it?

428
00:28:30,182 --> 00:28:32,015
Not just sea and dollar coffee.

429
00:28:32,055 --> 00:28:34,915
Every morning at five,
these same people set out

430
00:28:34,955 --> 00:28:37,633
stools, start over, and
find one for a guest.

431
00:28:41,760 --> 00:28:45,669
We don't know if Mai from the factory
went home or stayed in the city.

432
00:28:45,709 --> 00:28:48,490
But we know that this
morning in Ho Chi Minh City,

433
00:28:48,530 --> 00:28:50,633
someone set a stool by the road again.

434
00:28:50,673 --> 00:28:52,721
And if you come, one will be for you.

435
00:29:15,740 --> 00:29:18,244
Next time, we'll fly to the
other side of the world.

436
00:29:18,284 --> 00:29:22,060
Different lives, different streets, and
maybe no stools at all. See you there.
